{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## INDEPENDENT AC VERIFICATION (all 18) — 15 supportable, criterion 11 NOT TICKABLE AS WRITTEN, and\n## ONE LIVE CELL FOUND THAT CANNOT FAIL\n\nCommissioned a read-only pass over all 18 criteria against the LANDED tree rather than against this\nticket's own summaries of itself, because ticking a box asserts the item is done. Evidence base: the\nworktree tree plus the last recorded harness run 30772461871 (`main @ bd3de0f864`, 48 passed / 1 failed,\ncanary `collected=49 executed=49 skipped=0`). **That run predates the seven newest cells — the live\nsuite now collects 56, so SEVEN CELLS HAVE NEVER EXECUTED AGAINST THE HARNESS.** No live cell was run\nduring verification (arm64 host; the harness is amd64-only).\n\n### The finding that matters most: inbound cell `08-assign` CANNOT FAIL — both halves are vacuous\n\n`_in_assign` (test_dc_mutations.py:311-320) assigns `ADMIN_USER`; `_oracle_in_assign` (:323-334) asserts\nonly that `.assignee` is TRUTHY. But `bound_dc_issue`'s seeded issue arrives **already assigned to the\nproject lead (admin)** — this suite asserts that itself in two places (`_dc_support.py:204-212` and\n`test_dc_mutations.py:1517-1526`), and harness run 30763838558 demonstrated it by finding `jira/'admin'`\nalready mapped. So the mutation is a NO-OP and the oracle checks a field the binding pass had already\npopulated. The cell passes whether or not inbound assignee sync works at all.\n\nThis is this epic's signature failure class, found inside the very suite whose purpose is to end it —\nthe same shape as the vacuous assignee oracle that, once repaired, immediately surfaced real defect\n8d68 after months of green. The AC's row-8 inbound oracle is \"`.assignee` is the mapped identity\"; a\ntruthiness check is not that. **Being repaired** (see the remediation note below), using the pattern the\nMINT cell already established at :1466-1586 — establish ABSENCE first (`forget_identity_mapping` +\nassert `resolve_mapping(...) is None`), then assert the SPECIFIC mapped identity.\n\n### Criterion 11 (\"all 14 mutations x 2 directions\") — NOT TICKABLE AS WRITTEN. Three deviations.\n\n26 of the 28 named cells exist and were individually enumerated. The gaps:\n\n 1. **ROW 1 OUTBOUND IS ENTIRELY MISSING** — and it was NOT on this ticket's own list of known gaps.\n    The criterion requires asserting the DC issue carries the provenance label `rebar-id:<local_id>`\n    AND the entity property `local_id` read by raw REST.\n    `grep -rn \"rebar-id:\\|properties/local_id\" tests/external/` returns NOTHING. **Being added.**\n 2. **ROW 12 OUTBOUND (sub-task) is only implicit** — already self-recorded in comment 29: the parent\n    that row-13-outbound asserts present came from issue creation, not from a rebar push.\n 3. **ROW 14 INBOUND asserts a DIFFERENT oracle than the AC text, for a verified-correct reason.** The\n    AC demands `[.plan[] | select(direction==\"inbound\" and action==\"probe\" and target==KEY)] | length\n    == 1`. The code (:1340-1395) instead asserts \"no teardown entry, and the local ticket survives\".\n    The substitution is sound and was re-verified in source: `run_differs.py:234` passes\n    `ctx.prev_snapshot` as `local_state`; snapshot entries are keyed by jira_key and carry no\n    `jira_key` FIELD (`_snapshot_schema.py:26,99`); so `differ.py:641-651`'s `local_entry.get(\"jira_key\")`\n    is always falsy and **the probe can never fire in production**. The AC's oracle is unsatisfiable by\n    construction; the substitute is a legitimate ADR-0028 observable. Recording rather than quietly\n    ticking, because an AC that prescribes a MECHANISM can be wrong about the mechanism — the same\n    situation 8d68 hit.\n\n### Criteria 1-10 and 12-18: supportable, with these caveats recorded\n\n * **Criteria 2, 3 and 18 assert their own fixture's setup.** #2 (`REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=off`) and #3 (no\n   Cloud credential) read exactly the values `dc_store_copy_repo` sets/deletes moments earlier\n   (`conftest.py:620,624-625`); #3's hardcoded var list is IDENTICAL on both sides, so it cannot detect\n   a credential in the job environment, which is what the criterion claims it checks (and it misses\n   plausible names like `JIRA_TOKEN`). #18's file-content check compares the fixture-written\n   `rebar.toml` against the same `_BASE` expression that wrote it. **The isolation itself is real** —\n   the fixture genuinely sets the env and deletes the creds — so these are regression guards on the\n   FIXTURE, which has value; the criteria simply overclaim their reach. Filed as a follow-up rather\n   than blocking, because no product behaviour rests on them.\n * **Criterion 4 is correctly built** and is the one I most expected to be wrong: it compares the\n   filesystem entry SET under `.tickets-tracker/` against `.j11-expected-entries.json` derived from the\n   same `FETCH_HEAD` the archive came from — set equality, so a PARTIAL extraction fails, not a `> 0`\n   floor. It reads no `scanned` value anywhere. It also replaced the AC's hardcoded dot-file exclusion\n   list with a structural `is_ticket_entry` (`_dc_support.py:34-42`) because the enumerated list broke\n   on `.ensure-*` markers — a correct improvement, applied symmetrically to both sides.\n * **Criterion 13's pagination PRECONDITION is measured with the code under test** — :1660 uses\n   `dc_transport._paged_search(...)`, which IS the 9263 pagination fix, to decide whether the index has\n   settled. If it truncated again the cell would fail at :1665 saying \"the index is lagging ... **NOT a\n   pagination defect**\", actively misdirecting the next reader away from the defect the cell exists to\n   catch. **Being replaced** with a raw-REST count independent of the subject. The cell's actual\n   assertion (a SET intersection on `plan[].target`, `missing == []`, with `reconciler_page = 100` a\n   module-local literal) is sound and is not being touched.\n * **Two absence assertions read a FILTERED plan with no positive control** — idempotence (`mine == []`,\n   :1782) and row-14-inbound (`not teardown`, :1382). This story already burned a cycle on\n   `--filter-local-ids` being a post-filter that matched nothing (\"1640 mutations computed, 0 match\n   filter\", comment 4); under that failure both pass vacuously. Row 14 is partly saved by its second,\n   POSITIVE assertion (the local ticket survives with a non-teardown status). Filed as a follow-up.\n * **Criterion 12's \"after each direction\" pair-level `_assert_wrote_nothing`** (`test_reconcile_pass.py`\n   :256-265) runs over the EMPTY `dc_rebar_repo` — the exact \"converged because there was nothing to\n   reconcile\" shape this story exists to reject. Do not count it as idempotence evidence; the real\n   evidence is :1738-1786, which converges an outbound edit, confirms it landed on the instance, waits\n   for the index, and only THEN re-plans.\n * **Criterion 17 is thin**: every writing pass asserts `\"Traceback\" not in cp.stderr` (26 sites), but\n   the mutation cells never check the return code, and the dry-run passes in `_plan_entries_for`, the\n   pagination cell and the row-14 cell do not check `stderr` at all.\n * Smaller shape notes: `_out_status` (:439-445) mutates CONDITIONALLY (`if current != \"in_progress\"`),\n   so on an already-`in_progress` ticket it would assert a state it did not create (harmless today — an\n   imported DC issue lands `open`); row 3 (description, both directions) uses `in` containment where the\n   AC says the path EQUALS the value.\n * `.signing-key`, `.opcert-key` and `.opcert-key.pub` sit in the copied tracker and are not part of the\n   scrub. Not covered by any criterion; noted because the copy is a throwaway CI writes to.\n\n### What a harness run must show before criterion 11 and the round-trip criteria can be ticked\n\nGreen on `test_outbound_delete_link_round_trips`, `test_inbound_set_subtask_parent_round_trips`,\n`test_outbound_clear_parent_round_trips`, `test_outbound_delete_leaves_the_issue_absent_by_key_AND_by_id`,\nand — now that 751e's fix `205935bd72` is on main, mapping a blank assignee to `None` —\n`test_outbound_unassign_round_trips`. `test_inbound_delete_link_round_trips` (2b16) and\n`test_inbound_clear_parent_round_trips` (88d9) stay EXPECTED RED until those fixes land; **do not invert\nthem.** Both row-13 cells may instead report an explicit `SETUP FAILED`, which is the PLATFORM answer\n(DC refusing to null a sub-task's `fields.parent`), not a bridge verdict.\n\n### Remediation in flight\n\nAn agent is repairing, in an isolated worktree on `tests/external/live_jira_dc/` only: the `08-assign`\nvacuity, the missing row-1 outbound cell, and the pagination self-referential precondition. Because the\nharness cannot run on this host, each repaired oracle must additionally carry a `tests/unit/`\ndiscriminator test proving it FAILS on the wrong state and PASSES on the right one — the pattern\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_inbound_assignee_oracle_discriminates_5200.py` already established.\nFor `08-assign` that includes proving the OLD truthiness oracle passes on a state where the new one\nfails, which is the evidence the repair has teeth."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785728232598537001,"uuid":"7e50c533-1991-4693-b324-265a8212bddd"}